    wordCount: 295,
    text: `The rapid proliferation of artificial intelligence in corporate environments has generated considerable enthusiasm about productivity gains and cost reductions. However, organizations that rush to deploy AI systems without adequate forethought often encounter unforeseen consequences that can undermine their initial objectives.

One significant concern is the erosion of institutional knowledge. When companies automate tasks previously handled by experienced employees, they risk losing the tacit expertise that these workers accumulated over years. This implicit understanding—of client relationships, workflow nuances, and organizational culture—cannot be easily codified into algorithms. Once these employees are displaced, recovering their knowledge becomes prohibitively difficult.

Furthermore, the implementation of AI frequently exacerbates existing inequalities within organizations. Employees in lower-skilled positions are disproportionately affected by automation, while those with technical expertise see their leverage and compensation increase. This widening disparity can erode team cohesion and foster resentment among workers who perceive the technology as a threat rather than a tool.

The psychological toll on remaining employees is equally troubling. Constant surveillance through AI-powered monitoring systems, coupled with the perpetual anxiety of potential obsolescence, contributes to elevated stress levels and diminished job satisfaction. Research indicates that workplaces heavily reliant on algorithmic management experience higher turnover rates and lower employee engagement.

Moreover, the environmental footprint of AI infrastructure is frequently overlooked. The computational demands of training and running large language models require enormous quantities of electricity and water for cooling, contributing significantly to carbon emissions.

A prudent approach would involve implementing AI incrementally, with transparent communication about its role and limitations. Organizations must recognize that the most effective deployment of AI augments human capabilities rather than supplanting them entirely.`,
