    title: "Why Central Banks Struggle to Control Inflation in a Globalized Economy",
    difficulty: "TOEFL iBT+",
    wordCount: 305,
    text: `Central banks have long relied on interest rate adjustments as their primary tool for managing inflation. However, in an increasingly interconnected global economy, the effectiveness of domestic monetary policy has come under scrutiny. Several structural factors now constrain central banks' ability to control price levels independently.

Supply chain disruptions represent a particularly intractable challenge. The COVID-19 pandemic demonstrated how bottlenecks in distant manufacturing hubs could trigger rapid price increases in consumer goods worldwide. When inflation is driven by supply-side shocks rather than excessive domestic demand, raising interest rates may suppress economic activity without meaningfully reducing prices.

Currency dynamics further complicate the picture. In a world of free-flowing capital, interest rate decisions in one country can precipitate capital flight from another, destabilizing exchange rates and importing inflation through costlier imports. Emerging market economies are especially susceptible to these spillover effects, as their currencies often depreciate sharply when advanced economies tighten monetary policy.

The financialization of commodity markets adds another layer of complexity. Oil, wheat, and metals prices are increasingly determined by speculative trading rather than physical supply and demand fundamentals. Central banks have virtually no tools to counteract price volatility driven by financial speculation in global commodity exchanges.

Additionally, fiscal policy decisions by governments can either reinforce or undermine central bank efforts. Expansionary government spending during periods of tightening monetary policy creates contradictory signals that dilute the intended impact of interest rate increases.

Despite these limitations, central banks remain indispensable. Their credibility in maintaining inflation targets anchors public expectations, which itself moderates inflationary pressures. Nevertheless, addressing inflation in a globalized economy increasingly requires coordinated action among central banks, governments, and international regulatory bodies rather than unilateral monetary intervention.`,
